>
  <p>Yes, at the contract. Discover → execute → trace goes through one shared runtime contract and one product runtime. A host asks that runtime to run a capability. It does not become a second Traverse.</p>

  <p><strong>One shared <code>runtime.wasm</code></strong> means one orchestrator contract and ABI, and one product runtime. It does not mean every package literally ships a <code>.wasm</code> file you load.</p>

  <h2>Web loads the bundle file</h2>
  <p><code>traverse-embedder-web@0.14.0</code> loads <code>runtime/runtime.wasm</code> from the application bundle, checks it against <code>runtime/runtime.wasm.sha256</code>, and runs each capability through that orchestrator. The file is not inside the npm package. If either file is missing, or the digest does not match, <code>init</code> fails closed. The package README's Runtime-WASM execution section says <code>BundleEmbedder</code> digest-verifies that bundle orchestrator and drives the same host ABI native hosts use (<code>traverse_init</code>, <code>traverse_submit</code>, <code>traverse_next_event</code>). Node uses the same package with <code>NodeFsBundleLoader</code>. It still loads the bundle's own file.</p>

  <p>The public <a href="/discover.html">/discover</a> page is that web path. It executes a reviewed plan through a digest-verified nested <code>runtime.wasm</code>. The page cites Spec 1402 for that nested orchestrator file. Spec 1402, as the discover page uses it, names the file the browser loads. It is not a claim that the Rust crate ships the same file.</p>

  <h2>Rust links the runtime natively</h2>
  <p><code>traverse-embedder@0.14.0</code> on crates.io does not load a separate runtime-WASM file. Its <code>Cargo.toml</code> depends on <code>traverse-runtime</code> as a workspace dependency, so the crate links the Traverse runtime at the same workspace version. The crate README's Runtime-WASM compatibility section says the Rust package ships no separate runtime-WASM artifact.</p>

  <p>Capability artifacts in the bundle are still WASM. They have to target the Traverse host ABI that linked runtime supports. An unsupported schema, a digest mismatch, or an invalid manifest is rejected at <code>init</code> with a stable error code. The crate does not fall back to a <code>traverse-cli serve</code> sidecar.</p>

  <h2>Capabilities stay WASM. Hosts stay clients.</h2>
  <p>The business rule is a WASM capability artifact. The web package, the Rust crate, the CLI, and MCP are clients: UI and I/O around the same runtime contract. They do not reimplement the rule in the host language. Other published hosts can still load a digest-pinned <code>runtime.wasm</code>. The <a href="/platforms.html">Platforms</a> page describes the Swift clients that way. The Rust crate is the one that links <code>traverse-runtime</code> in-process instead.</p>

  <h2>Honest consumers</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>JS/TS</strong> — published npm <code>traverse-embedder-web@0.14.0</code>. Loads the bundle's <code>runtime/runtime.wasm</code>.</li>
    <li><strong>Rust</strong> — published crates.io <code>traverse-embedder@0.14.0</code>. Links <code>traverse-runtime</code> natively. No separate runtime-WASM artifact.</li>
    <li><strong>AI agents</strong> — published <code>traverse-mcp</code> (stdio bootstrap).</li>
    <li><strong>Python</strong> — shell out to <code>traverse-cli capability-package execute</code>. There is <strong>no Python SDK</strong>.</li>
    <li><strong>Swift / Kotlin / .NET</strong> — published, pre-1.0, and not Certified under Spec 529. Swift xcframework on <code>swift-host-v*</code> tags; Kotlin <code>com.traverse-framework:traverse-embedder</code> 0.14.0 on Maven Central; .NET <code>TraverseEmbedder</code> 0.14.0 on nuget.org.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>The published 0.14.0 Maven and NuGet packages do not include <code>ExactModelHost</code>. Check <a href="/platforms.html">Platforms</a> before treating a host as shipped.</p>
